# Which of your pieces is doing nothing?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What makes a piece good or bad: outposts, the bad bishop? Why improve your worst piece first? Why do rooks belong on open files and the seventh rank?"
next: {"title":"How do you make a plan in the middlegame?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/21-how-do-you-make-a-plan-in-the-middlegame.cast"}

## Warm-up: look behind, break through
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r7/6p1/1p6/3k4/8/6P1/5PKP/3B4 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bf3+, Ke5, Bxa8]}
      - {fen: 8/ppp5/8/PPP5/8/8/5k2/K7 w - - 0 1, moves: [b6, cxb6, a6, bxa6, c6]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: which of your pieces is doing nothing, and what to do about it. First, two warm-ups.
    draw position

B: His rook against my bishop. Bishop to b3 is check, at least.
    point at.ref sq_b3 gesture circle

A: A check, yes. But the king steps aside, and then what? Look behind the king.
    highlight piece_d5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Look for a skewer.","Check the king on the long diagonal."] themes ["skewer"] voice a
        right: Bishop to f3, check: the king has to leave the long diagonal, and the bishop takes the rook on a8.
        wrong: Which check has something standing behind the king?

B: So the rook was behind the king all along.
    highlight piece_a8

A: Second one: only pawns.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Three against three. Nobody gets through.
    highlight piece_a7 piece_b7 piece_c7

A: Your pawns are nearer the end. Give two, and the third runs.
    highlight piece_a5 piece_b5 piece_c5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Break through." widget chess line true hints ["Give up a pawn first.","The middle pawn goes first."] themes ["pawn structure"] voice a
        right: Pawn to b6: the c-pawn takes. Pawn to a6: the b-pawn takes. Then c6, and nothing can stop it reaching c8.
        wrong: Push the middle pawn: whichever pawn takes it, look at the gap it leaves.

B: I'd never have given two pawns away.
    highlight piece_c6

A: Every one of those pawns had a job. Today: the pieces that don't.
    highlight piece_a6 piece_b6 piece_c6

## Good pieces, bad pieces
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 2r2rk1/pp1qbppp/3p4/3Np3/4P3/3Q4/PPP2PPP/2R2RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxe7+, Qxe7], highlights: [d5, d6, e5]}
      - {fen: 2r2rk1/pp1qbppp/3p4/4p3/4P3/N2Q4/PPP2PPP/2R2RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [], arrows: [{from: a3, to: c4}, {from: c4, to: e3}, {from: e3, to: d5}], highlights: [d5]}
      - {fen: 2r3k1/pp3pp1/2p4p/4p3/4P3/2P4P/PP3PP1/5RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rd1, Kf8, Rd7, Rb8], highlights: [d1, d2, d3, d4, d5, d6, d7, d8]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: mysystem
      title: My System (Mein System)
      authors: Aron Nimzowitsch
      year: 1925
      finding: 'Chapter 2, Open Files: the main objective of a rook or queen on an open file is the eventual occupation of the 7th or 8th rank; chapter 3 is on the seventh and eighth ranks.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_file
    - id: outpost
      title: Outpost (chess)
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: An outpost is a square on the fourth to seventh rank, protected by a pawn, that no enemy pawn can attack.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outpost_(chess)

    card title "Good pieces, bad pieces"

A: Now that the warm-up is done: White's knight on d5. Is it a good piece or a bad one?
    draw position

B: Knight takes e7, check! A capture and a check.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: And the queen takes back. Material is level, but you swapped your best piece for his worst.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

B: But a knight and a bishop are both worth three.
    highlight piece_e7

A: On paper. On the board, look where they stood.
    animate plies_shown 0 duration 1.2

A: No black pawn can attack d5 any more:
    draw hl_d5

A: his c-pawn is gone,
    point at.ref sq_c7 gesture circle

A: and the e-pawn has gone past.
    highlight piece_e5

A: A safe square in his half, guarded by your pawn: an outpost.
    highlight piece_e4

A: Black's bishop is the opposite.
    highlight piece_e7

A: His own pawns on d6 and e5 stand on its colour,
    draw hl_d6 hl_e5 parallel true

A: and wall it in: a bad bishop.
    highlight piece_e7

B: So keep the knight, and leave him the bad bishop.
    highlight piece_d5 piece_e7

A: Bad isn't useless: it still guards d6. But it attacks nothing.
    blocking false

    point at.ref piece_d6 gesture tap

    pause 1.2

    highlight piece_e7 duration 2.5

    erase hl_d5 hl_d6 hl_e5

A: A new position. It's quiet: nothing hangs on either side.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Then I attack. Pawn to g4, and the pawns roll at his king.
    point at.ref sq_g4 gesture circle

A: Pawn to g4 just drops a pawn: his queen on d7 takes it.
    highlight piece_d7

A: Before you attack, ask which of your pieces is doing nothing.
    highlight piece_a3 piece_d3 piece_c1 piece_f1

A: Which White piece is doing least?
    highlight piece_a3 piece_d3

    quiz "Which White piece is doing least?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 950 buttons_at.y 380 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: The knight on a3: on the rim, it reaches nothing that matters.
        wrong: Which piece attacks nothing?
        + Knight
        * Queen

    highlight piece_a3 duration 1.2

A: Three jumps: c4, e3, d5. Black may try to swap it off, but he has to find that.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 arrow_2 hl_d5

A: When nothing is forcing, improving your worst piece is a simple plan.
    highlight piece_a3

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1 arrow_2 hl_d5

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Rooks want open files: files with no pawns at all.
    highlight piece_f1

B: Rook to e1, next to my king's pawn.
    point at.ref sq_e1 gesture circle

A: Where should the rook on f1 go?
    highlight piece_f1

    quiz "Where should the rook on f1 go?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 950 buttons_at.y 380 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: d1: the only file with no pawns, so the rook sees all the way to d8. On e1 it would stare at its own pawn.
        wrong: Which file has no pawns on it?
        + d1
        * e1
        * f1

    draw hl_d1 hl_d2 hl_d3 hl_d4 hl_d5 hl_d6 hl_d7 hl_d8 parallel true

A: Rook to d1. Black can't fight for the file: rook to d8 just loses his rook.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

    erase hl_d1 hl_d2 hl_d3 hl_d4 hl_d5 hl_d6 hl_d7 hl_d8

B: Say he brings his king over.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Now the rook goes to the seventh rank, where his pawns still stand at home.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: His rook has to guard b7. For now, it's his piece doing nothing.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: It's an old rule. Aron Nimzowitsch wrote in 1925, in My System,
    portrait nimzo cites ["mysystem"] x 95 y 540 width 165 of "Aron Nimzowitsch"

A: that a rook on an open file is aiming for the seventh or eighth rank.
    highlight piece_d7

    erase nimzo

A: So find your worst piece, and give it a better square: an outpost, an open file, the seventh rank.
    highlight piece_d7 piece_b8

## Your turn: put them to work
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. Your pieces all had jobs.
    good: Most of them. The misses come back in review.
    poor: Rooks and knights take a few games to place. Try them again tomorrow.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r4rk1/3q1p1p/pb1p2p1/1p1Np3/4P3/P7/1PP1QPPP/5RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nf6+, Kg7, Nxd7]}
      - {fen: 5rk1/2R1R1pp/8/8/8/1q5P/5PP1/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxg7+, Kh8, Rxh7+, Kg8, Rcg7#]}
      - {fen: r5k1/5ppp/8/8/8/1q5P/3R1PP1/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rd8+, Rxd8, Rxd8#]}
      - {fen: 2r3k1/pb1R1ppp/1p6/8/8/4PN1P/P4PP1/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxb7]}
      - {fen: 7b/p5p1/1p1k1p1p/3NpP2/1P2P1PP/P2K4/8/8 w - - 0 1}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you've seen good and bad pieces: your turn. Four puzzles, then a game against the computer.
    draw position

A: First, a knight on its outpost.
    highlight piece_d5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Look for a fork with check.","The knight on d5."] themes ["outpost","fork"] voice a test d20
        right: Knight to f6, check: it hits the king and the queen on d7. The king steps away, and the knight takes the queen.
        wrong: Which knight jump gives check and hits something else too?

B: From d5, it reached them both.
    highlight piece_d7

A: Two rooks on the seventh rank. Black has a queen; you don't.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in three." widget chess line true hints ["Start with a capture that checks.","Rook takes g7."] themes ["mating pattern"] voice a test d20
        right: Rook takes g7, check. The king goes to h8; rook takes h7, check; back to g8; and the other rook comes to g7: mate.
        wrong: Both rooks work along the seventh rank. Start with a capture that checks.

B: They ate the pawns in front of his king.
    highlight piece_g7 piece_h7

A: Now two rooks on the open file.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the best move." widget chess line true hints ["Look at his back rank.","One rook goes in first."] themes ["open file","back rank"] voice a test d20
        right: Rook to d8, check: his rook takes, and your second rook takes back with mate on the back rank.
        wrong: His king has no escape square. Which check lands on his back rank?

A: Last one. A rook on the seventh, and a bishop on b7.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Can I just take it? It looks too easy.
    highlight piece_b7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the best move." widget chess line true hints ["Check what guards b7.","Nothing does."] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a test d20
        right: Rook takes b7: nothing guards the bishop. Sometimes the obvious move is the right one.
        wrong: Count what guards the bishop on b7.

    hide position

A: You scored {d20.score} of {d20.total}.
    results test d20

    show position

A: Now against the computer.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Your knight has its outpost; his bishop is buried behind its own pawns.
    highlight piece_d5 piece_h8

B: It can't even move.
    highlight piece_g7

A: It can never touch a light square, so walk your king in on them. Win a pawn within fifteen moves.
    explore activity vs_computer goal.kind win_material goal.within 15 goal.amount 1 store g20

    hide position

A: Every piece wants a job: the knight an outpost,
    text j_knight "knight: outpost" x 500 y 500 font_size 68

A: the rook an open file,
    text j_rook "rook: open file" x 500 y 385 font_size 68

A: and the bishop pawns off its colour.
    text j_bishop "bishop: free diagonals" x 500 y 270 font_size 68

    erase j_knight j_rook j_bishop

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 380 font_size 120

## Where next
end_page: true

